# ayywi

Look up a component before using it — via the ayywi MCP server (`get_component`) if it's configured, else `node_modules/@danitesler/ayywi/llms/<component>.md` (markdown) or `node_modules/@danitesler/ayywi/manifest/components.json` (JSON). Each lists classes, variants, React props, custom element, accessibility notes, do/don't and copy-ready examples; `llms-full.txt` has every rule and component API in one file, without examples. Run `npx @danitesler/ayywi lint` when you're done.

Installing and updating ayywi:
- Install from npm: `pnpm add @danitesler/ayywi` (or npm/yarn), then `npx @danitesler/ayywi init`. Import only from "@danitesler/ayywi" and its subpaths ("/react", "/elements", "/css", "/fonts.css", "/brands/*", "/fonts/*", "/icons/*"). Never from "ayywi": that name is only the CLI. Never reach into node_modules/@danitesler/ayywi/dist by path.
- Never vendor ayywi: no copy of its dist/ in the repo, no .tgz, no file: or link: dependency in a committed package.json. A fresh clone must install with the package manager alone.
- To change ayywi while building an app, point the app's bundler at a local ayywi checkout only for that session (a Vite resolve.alias read from an AYYWI_DIR environment variable, with resolve.dedupe for react and react-dom), and run `pnpm build` in the checkout after each change. Never commit that link.
- Upgrade with `pnpm up @danitesler/ayywi`, then `npx @danitesler/ayywi init --force` so the skill, rules and AGENTS.md section match the installed version, and read the CHANGELOG between the two versions.
- When ayywi has no component or variant for something, compose it from ayywi pieces in the app, mark it with a `// ayywi-candidate: <what it is>` comment, and add it to ayywi itself when a second screen or app needs it. Never fork, copy or restyle an ayywi component inside the app.

1. Use existing ayywi classes/components and their variants before writing any custom CSS. Never rebuild one under your own name (a .x-chip, .x-card, a <button> styled by hand) or restyle one: your class next to its ayy- class sets only layout (margin, size, position). If nothing fits, compose ayywi pieces. Never add a second UI kit. Never write a raw native control (<button>, <select>, <input>, <textarea>, <table>, <dialog>, <details>) or a <div>/<span> with a click handler: each is an ayywi component. Before writing a screen, name the component for every piece by what it has to do (the Need → component map, or the MCP pick_component tool).
2. Never hardcode colours (hex, rgb, hsl, named) — in stylesheets or in inline styles. Use var(--ayy-color-*) tokens; for tints use color-mix(in srgb, var(--ayy-color-text) N%, transparent) or the wash/line tokens.
3. Use logical properties only: margin-inline-start, padding-inline, inset-inline-end, text-align: start, inline-size. Never left/right/margin-left/padding-right, so RTL works.
4. Spacing, sizes, radius, type and motion come from tokens (--ayy-space-*, --ayy-size-*, --ayy-radius-*, --ayy-text-*, --ayy-weight-*, --ayy-leading-*, --ayy-control-*, --ayy-shadow-*, --ayy-ease-*, --ayy-duration-*). Control sizes follow data-density — don't hardcode heights.
5. Theme with data-theme="dark|light|dark-contrast|light-gray" (or nothing = follow OS). Never write separate dark-mode colours; tokens already switch. To restyle, override semantic tokens rather than editing components.
6. Brand a product with a generated brand, never hand-picked colours: `npx @danitesler/ayywi brand <#seed> --name <name>` (or createBrand()/setBrand() from "@danitesler/ayywi", or the MCP create_brand tool) makes the colour scale, primary, primary-fg and ring for every theme with contrast checked, plus fonts and corners (--shape pill|round|soft|sharp). Load its CSS and set data-brand. Colour beyond primary comes from --ayy-brand-50…950 (the scale) for illustrations and brand moments, never on navigation or page backgrounds.
7. Every interactive element keeps its visible :focus-visible ring. Icon-only buttons need aria-label. Form controls need a label. State lives in native/ARIA attributes (disabled, checked, aria-selected, aria-current, aria-expanded, aria-invalid, aria-busy, open).
8. Toolbar items and icon-only buttons get a Tooltip explaining their action on hover and keyboard focus, paired with a keyboard hint element (<Shortcut keys="..." /> in React, .ayy-shortcut in HTML) whenever a shortcut exists. Never use native title="" attributes.
9. Every component's spec lists its states (default, hover, pressed, focus, disabled, selected, error, loading, plus its own such as open) and its sizes, in manifest/components.json, llms/<slug>.md and get_component. Reach a state only through the attribute or prop its spec names. Where a state says it doesn't apply, do what it says instead; never style a state a component doesn't have.
10. Stateful styles need a @media (forced-colors: active) fallback (Windows High Contrast erases fills).
11. Prefer animating transform and opacity (use a logical property like inset-inline-start only when the motion must follow text direction). Everything must still work under prefers-reduced-motion — base.css collapses ayy animations. Scroll-in motion comes from .ayy-reveal, not a script; nothing loops unless it shows a live state. Movement on :hover (lifts, zooms, spins) goes inside @media (hover: hover), because a tap leaves :hover stuck on touch screens.
12. The frame is monochrome; colour comes from the content. Set --ayy-spot (to an --ayy-accent-* token) on the card or page that shows the content, and the spotlight, contents bar, icon tiles and .ayy-accent-text inside pick it up. Never tint the navbar, sidebar, buttons or page background per page.
13. Websites follow one anatomy: .ayy-skip-link, a Navbar (with a NavbarToggle so its links fold into a menu on phones), then <main> at .ayy-container width (fading Separators between marketing sections), then the Footer. The page's call to action is a ring button (the navbar's repeats the same action); everything else outline or ghost.
14. Apps (anything signed-in) follow one frame: .ayy-skip-link, then the App shell — a sidebar on wide screens; on phones an __bar (brand plus one or two actions) and a Bottom nav with the sidebar's top destinations, same icons and order (four and a More tab when there are more). Each screen in its main starts with a Page header: the one h1, a line of description, at most one primary button; on phones a screen pushed from a list starts with a Top bar (back, title, actions) instead, and a screen's one main create action can be a Floating action button. Lists of rows on phones get Swipe actions for their two or three most common actions, which are also reachable elsewhere. Settings open over the app in a near-full-screen Dialog (size="full") holding the App shell in settings mode (the Settings pattern: a Settings title and the sections in its sidebar; one section of Settings rows in its main; on phones the dialog fills the screen and the section list and a section are two screens), never in a small or side dialog; desktop apps add a Command palette on ⌘K. Never put a Navbar in an app.
15. An app screen fills the main. Don't wrap it in .ayy-container or a max-inline-size — a max-inline-size on an example only sizes that example in the docs, it is not page layout. Long text (a description, .ayy-prose) may stop at --ayy-size-measure. Settings mode already centres its column; don't add a second cap.
16. Every list, table and page has three more states: loading (Skeleton in the shape of the content with aria-busy; a Spinner or <Button loading> for an action), empty (an Empty state with the action that fills it) and error (an Alert with a retry for a failed load or save; FieldError next to a field).
17. Data views use ayywi's own pieces: Chart (BarChart, LineChart, DonutChart, ScatterChart, HeatmapChart, FunnelChart and the rest), Bar list and Sparkline for charts, coloured from --ayy-chart-1… in order; filters as Chips (with counts) above the results and the active ones as removable chips; sortable Table columns with a .ayy-table__sort button and aria-sort on the <th>. With a chart library, colour it with var(--ayy-chart-N) (SVG) or chartColors() and chartTheme() (canvas); never its default palette.
18. Pick form controls by the choice: a Segmented control for two to five short options, Choice cards when each option needs a sentence or a price (compact for time slots), a Select for a short list, a Combobox for many known values, a Transfer list to move items between available and chosen lists, a PIN input for verification codes or PINs, Mentions for inline @mentions or #references inside a textarea, a Rating for 1–5 star scores, a Number field for small counts, a Slider for a rough amount, a DatePicker or DatePickerGrid for a date near today (DateRange / DateRangePicker for a date span, TimePicker / TimePickerTrigger for stepped times, DateTimePicker / DateTimePickerTrigger for date and time, native type="date" for a birth date, type="time" for rapid typing), ColorSwatches for a fixed palette or ColorPicker for any custom colour, a Tag input for free-text tags or recipients, a Search bar to filter a list, a Shortcut recorder for key bindings, File upload for files.
19. Don't write breakpoints for navigation or layout: App shell, Navbar and Pagination switch at 48rem on their own, and .ayy-grid and .ayy-split adapt to their container. If you truly need one, use 48rem (phone) or 64rem (tablet).
20. Every <img> gets width and height (its real size) so nothing jumps while it loads. Product screenshots go in a Frame. Draw small product visuals as SVG with tokens instead of images with text baked in.
21. No :dir() selectors — minifiers rewrite them into :lang() lists that ignore dir="rtl". Logical properties make direction checks unnecessary.
22. Body text must fall back to system fonts — never remove the system-ui stack from --ayy-font-body (CJK/Arabic/Cyrillic rely on it).
23. Icons come from Hugeicons (@hugeicons/core-free-icons): <Icon icon={Search01Icon} /> in React, iconSvg(Search01Icon) or the pasted SVG with class="ayy-icon" elsewhere. Don't add another icon set: to draw ayywi's own icons, and the common ones you name, in Lucide, Tabler, Material Symbols or Feather, import @danitesler/ayywi/icons/<pack>.css and call setIconPack() (or set data-ayy-icons). Icons are decorative (aria-hidden) unless you give them a label; arrows that point along the reading direction take directional.
24. In React import from "@danitesler/ayywi/react"; in other frameworks and plain HTML use the class names, the class helpers from "@danitesler/ayywi" (buttonClass…) and the custom elements from "@danitesler/ayywi/elements" (<ayy-app-shell>, <ayy-navbar>, <ayy-tabs>, <ayy-combobox>, <ayy-date-picker>, <ayy-date-range>, <ayy-time-picker>, <ayy-date-time-picker>, <ayy-color-picker>, <ayy-icon-picker>, <ayy-toolbar>, <ayy-action-bar>, <ayy-shortcut-recorder>, <ayy-command>, <ayy-tag-input>, <ayy-record-picker>, <ayy-pin-input>, <ayy-mentions>, <ayy-block-editor>, <ayy-transfer-list>, <ayy-inline-edit>, <ayy-swipe>, <ayy-drag-drop>, <ayy-dialog>, <ayy-menu>, <ayy-popover>, <ayy-tooltip>, <ayy-toc>, <ayy-tree>, <ayy-carousel>, <ayy-table>, <ayy-grid>).
25. Run `npx @danitesler/ayywi lint` after UI changes and fix every finding it reports, warnings included: raw-control, bare-control, clickable-element and rebuilt-component mean an ayywi component was skipped.

Need → component (name one for every piece before you write it; MCP `pick_component` answers the same):
- Run an action (save, create, delete, open): Button: one primary per view, outline/ghost for the rest, destructive for delete, size icon + aria-label for icon-only; SplitButton + DropdownMenu when one default action has secondary alternatives. Not <button> with your own classes or none, a <div>/<span> with onClick, a link styled as a button for an action.
- Choose one value from a list (theme, country, role, sort order): Select for a list (it opens ayywi's own options list; touch screens keep the OS picker), Segmented control for two to five short options shown at once, Combobox for many values people type to find. Not a raw <select>, a button that opens a hand-built <div> of choices, a Dropdown menu used as a picker.
- Choose between options that each need a sentence, an icon or a price (plans, delivery, time slots): ChoiceGroup of ChoiceCards (compact for time slots, scroll for a day strip). Not Cards with onClick and a selected class.
- Switch between views or ranges of the same content (list/grid, 7d/30d): SegmentedControl, two to five options. Not a row of buttons with aria-pressed or an active class.
- Show one of several panels of content: Tabs (TabsList, TabsTrigger, TabsContent). Not a Segmented control or buttons that swap panels.
- Turn a setting on or off (a toggle: notifications, dark mode, auto-save): Switch when it applies at once; Checkbox in a form that's submitted, or to agree to something. Not <input type="checkbox"> unstyled, a toggle drawn with divs.
- Pick one of a few options in a form: Radio group; a Segmented control when the options are short. Not raw <input type="radio">.
- Filter a list or tag things: Chips (checkbox, radio or ChipButton, with counts) above the results, the active ones as removable chips; Tag input for free-text tags or recipients. Not pills built from <span>s or buttons with an active class.
- Move items between two lists (available vs assigned permissions, columns or members): TransferList (<ayy-transfer-list> in HTML) with searchable Available and Selected panes and Move selected / Move all buttons. Not two custom list boxes with hand-wired state and arrow buttons.
- Filter a list, table or feed (status tabs, faceted dropdowns, active chips): FilterBar with quick filter tabs (FilterBarTabs), faceted dropdown triggers (FilterBarTrigger) with count badges, and active filter chips with a Clear all button. Not a row of custom buttons with active classes, hand-built filter bars.
- Sort a list, table or feed by field and direction: Sorting with SortingTrigger opening a DropdownMenu of sort options, or sortable Table column headers. Not a button that flips a state with no accessible label, hand-drawn sort arrows.
- Type text, an email, a number or a password: Field + Label + Input (Input group for an icon or a unit, Textarea for long text, Number field for small counts), FieldHint and FieldError under it. Not a raw <input>/<textarea>, a placeholder instead of a label.
- Enter a verification code, OTP or PIN: PinInput (<ayy-pin-input> in HTML) inside a Field, with group for a split 3+3 code and mask for secret PINs. Not six separate <input maxlength="1"> elements wired by hand or a single plain text input.
- Write or edit a rich document: notes, a page, a description with headings, lists, to-dos, images and embeds: BlockEditor (<ayy-block-editor> in HTML): Notion-style blocks with / for block types, Markdown shortcuts, a formatting bar, @ mentions and drag handles; store its value (blocks) or convert with blockEditorToMarkdown / blockEditorToHtml. Not a <textarea> with a Markdown preview, execCommand on one big contenteditable, or a third-party rich text library.
- Mention people or reference issues inline in a comment (@user, #issue): Mentions (<ayy-mentions> in HTML) wrapping a textarea and suggestion listbox. Not contenteditable rich-text hacks or an unanchored dropdown under a textarea.
- Give or display a star rating or review score: Rating (interactive radio stars with optional half and clearable, or readOnly for fractional scores). Not buttons or spans of ★ characters with custom click handlers.
- Search or filter by typing: Search bar to filter what's on screen, Combobox to pick a value, Command palette (⌘K) to jump anywhere. Not an <input> with a hand-drawn magnifier.
- Pick a date or time: DatePicker or DatePickerGrid for one date near today; DateRange or DateRangePicker for a start and end span; TimePicker or TimePickerTrigger for discrete hours and minutes; DateTimePicker or DateTimePickerTrigger for combined date and time; Input type="date" for a birth date, type="time" for rapid keyboard entry. Not a hand-built month grid or time dropdown.
- Pick an icon or emoji: IconPicker (<ayy-icon-picker>) in a Popover or a menu's submenu panel: search, a grid of Hugeicons and emoji, and a remove button. Not a menu with one item per icon, a hand-built emoji grid.
- Pick a colour: ColorSwatches for a fixed set of choices, ColorSwatchCustom or ColorPicker for any custom colour with hue/alpha/hex. Not a raw <input type="color">, coloured buttons.
- Set an amount or a range: Slider / SliderRange for a rough amount or a price range, Number field for exact small counts. Not a raw <input type="range">.
- Upload files: Dropzone (compact in a form), then a List divided of the files with a Progress each. Not a raw <input type="file">, a dashed div.
- Drag and drop reordering, board or kanban: Sortable with SortableItem and DragHandle (or DragDropArea with DropTargets and <ayy-drag-drop> in HTML). Not a third-party dnd library, raw HTML5 dragstart handlers.
- Record a keyboard shortcut or show one: Shortcut recorder to set one; Kbd / Shortcut to show one. Not a <kbd> or <span> styled by hand.
- Show a code snippet, command or config file: CodeBlock with CodeBlockHeader, CodeBlockFilename, CodeBlockCopy, CodeBlockPre and CodeBlockCode (numbered for line numbers, .ayy-token-* for syntax tokens). Not a raw <pre> with custom background and copy button.
- Show activity history, audit log or chronological events: Timeline of TimelineItems (TimelineMarker with pulse on live events, TimelineTime, TimelineTitle, TimelineBody; alternate for wide milestone pages). Not divs with a border-left line and absolute dots.
- Show more actions behind a button (⋯, a user menu): DropdownMenu of items; destructive item last. Not a hand-built absolutely positioned <div>.
- Ask to confirm, or collect a few fields over the page: Dialog (DialogContent, title, body, footer with the primary action last). Not window.confirm(), a fixed <div> with a backdrop.
- A side panel, drawer or bottom sheet: Dialog with side="end", "start" or "bottom". Not a custom fixed div that slides in.
- Settings for an app: The Settings pattern: a full-size Dialog holding the App shell in settings mode, Settings groups of SettingsRow / SettingsLink. Not a settings page with Tabs, a small dialog, rows built from divs.
- Small extra content on click (details, a mini form): Popover. Not a hand-positioned div.
- Explain an icon button on hover or focus: Tooltip (the button keeps its aria-label); add a Shortcut inside for its keyboard hint. Not title="" or a CSS-only bubble.
- Confirm something happened, or offer undo: toast.success() / toast() with an action. Not a hand-built fixed banner.
- Show a capture, video or file preview with quick corner actions: toast.card() or CardToast for screen captures, video thumbnails and quick actions. Not a custom corner popup, fixed floating card without a toaster stack.
- Tell people about a problem or a state on the page: Alert (destructive with a retry for a failed load or save). Not a coloured <div>.
- Show loading: Skeleton in the shape of the content (aria-busy on the region), Spinner for a small area, Button loading for an action, Progress for a known amount. Not "Loading…" text, a hand-drawn spinner.
- Nothing to show yet, or no results: Empty state with the action that fills it (compact inside a card or table). Not a centred muted paragraph.
- Group content in a box: Card with CardHeader / CardTitle / CardDescription, CardContent, CardFooter; CardLink when the whole card opens something. Not a <div> with a border, radius and padding.
- Rows of things (members, files, threads, settings): List (ListLink for clickable rows, divided for settings-style rows); Settings for preference rows; Swipe actions on phones. Not <ul>/<div> rows with your own borders and hover, <li onClick>.
- Records compared across columns: Table (sortable headers, a select column, TableRowActions with inline quick buttons and a ⋯ DropdownMenu), ActionBar when rows are selected, Pagination under it. Not a raw <table>, a CSS grid of divs.
- Captures, recordings or pictures to open, with their details and actions on hover (a screenshot history, a media library): Cards of the view type (Card view) in a .ayy-grid: CardViewMedia (a button or link that opens it) with CardViewImage letterboxed over a blurred copy, CardViewBadge for a recording's length, CardViewBody for title, details and badges, and grouped HoverActions with a ⋯ DropdownMenu. Use Grid instead when people select several. Not a plain Card with CardMedia and an <img>, a hand-built gradient caption, absolutely positioned buttons that appear on hover.
- Icon actions over a picture, a video frame or a card (copy, annotate, pin, delete): HoverActions in a corner (position) of the picture's box, reveal to show them on hover and focus, group to put them on one glass bar; each HoverAction has a label that is also its tooltip. Not ghost buttons restyled with a translucent background, title="" tooltips, buttons that only appear on :hover with no keyboard way to reach them.
- Selectable cards of media assets, screenshots, social posts, projects or user records: Grid of GridItems (GridItemSelect checkbox, GridItemActions with inline quick actions and a ⋯ DropdownMenu, GridItemMedia for previews), ActionBar when items are selected. Not Cards with custom onClick selection state or hand-built floating checkboxes.
- Run bulk actions on selected table rows or grid items (edit, duplicate, copy, archive, delete): ActionBar (open when selection count > 0) with ActionBarSelection, ActionBarCount, ActionBarClose and ActionBarActions of ActionBarButtons (destructive last). Not a hand-built fixed bottom bar or static buttons above the table.
- A number that matters (KPI): Card + Stat + a status Badge, a Sparkline beside it. Not big bold text in a div.
- A chart: BarChart (grouped, stacked, 100%, horizontal, with lines), LineChart (area, stacked, step), HistogramChart, WaterfallChart, ScatterChart (bubbles), DonutChart (pie), GaugeChart, RadarChart, HeatmapChart, FunnelChart, BarList, Sparkline, coloured from --ayy-chart-1… in order. Not a chart library's default palette, divs as bars.
- A status, count or label on something: Badge (status variants). Not a coloured <span> pill.
- Show live status, online presence or server health as a compact dot: StatusDot (neutral, success, warning, destructive, info variants; pulse for live activity). Not a coloured <span> or <div> with border-radius: 50%, hand-drawn ping keyframes.
- A person or a team: Avatar (image with initials fallback), AvatarGroup. Not a round <img> or initials in a div.
- Collapsible sections or FAQ: Accordion (single to keep one open). Not a raw <details>, a div toggled by a button.
- A multi-step flow: Steps above the form, one primary Continue per step. Not numbered circles drawn by hand.
- The frame of an app: App shell (sidebar, a bar and a Bottom nav on phones), each screen starting with a Page header. Not a hand-built sidebar, header or bottom tab bar.
- The top of a website: Navbar with a NavbarToggle so links fold into a menu on phones. Not a <header> of links with a hand-built burger menu.
- Where you are, or which page of results: Breadcrumb for nested pages, Pagination for pages of results. Not links separated by slashes, numbered buttons.
- A screen pushed from a list on a phone: Top bar (back, title, actions). Not a hand-built header with a back arrow.
- A screenshot or picture of the product: Frame around it. Not an <img> with a border and shadow.
- Preview UI across responsive viewports with device switcher: Showcase with ShowcaseHeader, ShowcaseTitle, ShowcaseActions, ShowcaseStage, and ShowcaseViewport (desktop, tablet, mobile). Not hand-built iframe wrappers or ad-hoc responsive preview containers.
- Light/dark switch: Dropdown menu of themes (radio items) that calls setTheme(); an icon trigger shows the current one. Inside settings, use the theme picker of the Appearance need instead. Not a button that flips a class.
- Appearance settings: theme, density, text size, brand colour, corners, font, reduce motion, language or direction: In the Settings pattern's Appearance section: a Select with pictures for each choice: a SettingsScreen for Theme, Corners and Text direction, variant="rows" for Density, variant="brand" for Brand colour (a Custom option opens the colour picker), and "Aa" samples for Font and Text size; Switch for reduce motion (data-motion="reduce"); Select for language; a destructive SettingsLink to reset. Apply at once with setTheme(), setDensity(), setBrand(). Not a theme Select or a plain radio list, hand-drawn theme thumbnails or screenshots, a raw <input type="color">, Choice cards with only text, a button opening a popover of choices, a Slider for text size, a SegmentedControl for direction, swatches in the row for brand colour.
